@page "/Desktop"
@inject MatDesign.Web.Studio.DesignData Design
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Desktop and services";
    ViewData["PageSub"] = "Programs that are not web pages: a program with windows, its icon in the tray, its setup, and the agents that run as a service on a machine. They look and behave like the web apps, with the rules of four areas of their own.";
    ViewData["Crumbs"] = Crumbs.Of(("Design", "/"), ("Desktop and services", null));
    var sample = DesktopFigures.Sample(Design);
    var areas = new (string Area, string Page, string Href)[]
    {
        ("DESK", "Desktop app", "/Desktop/App"),
        ("TRAY", "Tray", "/Desktop/Tray"),
        ("SETUP", "Setup", "/Desktop/Setup"),
        ("AGENT", "Agents", "/Desktop/Agents"),
    };
}

<section class="mat-section" aria-labelledby="areas-title">
    <div class="mat-section__head">
        <h2 id="areas-title">Four areas of the rulebook</h2>
        <p class="mat-section__lead">Each has a page with its rules, drawn examples and, where they exist, pictures of programs that follow them.</p>
    </div>
    <ul class="doc-index">
        @foreach (var (areaId, title, href) in areas)
        {
            var area = Design.Area(areaId);
            var count = Design.RulesIn(areaId).Count();
            <li>
                <a class="doc-index__row" href="@href">
                    <span class="doc-index__key"><strong class="doc-index__title">@title</strong></span>
                    <span class="doc-index__body">
                        <span class="mat-muted">@area?.Summary</span>
                        <span class="mat-muted doc-fs-xs">@areaId · @count @(count == 1 ? "rule" : "rules")</span>
                    </span>
                </a>
            </li>
        }
    </ul>
</section>

<section class="mat-section" aria-labelledby="which-title">
    <div class="mat-section__head">
        <h2 id="which-title">Which rules a program follows</h2>
        <p class="mat-section__lead">A desktop program has windows of its own and usually a tray; an agent does its work as a service and shows itself only through its tray and its setup.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="mat-table-wrap">
        <table class="mat-table">
            <caption class="mat-sr-only">The areas of the rules that apply to each kind of program</caption>
            <thead>
                <tr><th scope="col">Program</th><th scope="col">Desktop app</th><th scope="col">Tray</th><th scope="col">Setup</th><th scope="col">Agents</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td data-label="Program"><strong>A desktop program</strong></td>
                    <td data-label="Desktop app">All of them</td>
                    <td data-label="Tray">When it has a tray</td>
                    <td data-label="Setup">Per user, no rights</td>
                    <td data-label="Agents">No</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td data-label="Program"><strong>An agent or a service</strong></td>
                    <td data-label="Desktop app">Its windows: the tokens, the icons, the keyboard</td>
                    <td data-label="Tray">All of them</td>
                    <td data-label="Setup">Administrator rights once</td>
                    <td data-label="Agents">All of them</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="mat-section" aria-labelledby="same-title">
    <div class="mat-section__head">
        <h2 id="same-title">The same design as the web</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="mat-prose">
        <p>A program on the desktop is the same product as its web app. It takes its colours, radii, spacing and type from the <a href="/Foundations/Tokens">tokens</a> (<a href="/Rules/Detail?id=DESK-02">DESK-02</a>), its icons from the <a href="/Foundations/Icons">sprite</a> (<a href="/Rules/Detail?id=DESK-09">DESK-09</a>) and its menu from the <a href="/Shell">shell</a> (<a href="/Rules/Detail?id=DESK-04">DESK-04</a>), in light and dark mode. What the web does not have is here: the title bar, the tray, the setup, the service.</p>
        <p>A toolkit for desktop programs, with the theme, the frame of the windows and the controls as one library, is planned. Until it exists, a program writes the values of the tokens into its theme file and generates its icons from the sprite with a script of its own.</p>
        <p>The names in the drawings on these pages are examples: the agent of <strong>@sample.Product</strong> is called <strong>@sample.Agent</strong>.</p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="mat-section" aria-labelledby="pictures-title">
    <div class="mat-section__head">
        <h2 id="pictures-title">Pictures of the windows</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="mat-prose">
        <p>Every program can draw its windows into picture files without a screen, from fixed sample data and never from the data of the machine it runs on: a test that renders the windows headless, or a command such as <code>@(sample.Slug)-agent snapshots &lt;folder&gt;</code>. The pictures in the documentation and on these pages are made that way, so they can be made again after every change, in light and in dark mode.</p>
    </div>
</section>
